# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '2 Samuel'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'David’s Reign, the Covenant Promise, and the Cost of His Sin'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '2 Samuel follows David from his anointing over Judah and then over all Israel, to the establishment of his kingdom and the promise of an enduring house. It records the LORD’s covenant with David through Nathan, David’s sin with Bathsheba and Nathan’s rebuke, the troubles of his house, David’s song of praise, and the numbering of the people and the purchase of the threshingfloor (2 Sam. 5:3; 7:12–16; 11:27; 12:7, 13; 22:2; 24:24).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD establishes David’s house and kingdom over Israel in the land (2 Sam. 5:12; 7:16).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 2 Samuel', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — David Becomes King (Chs. 1–5)'), ('p', 'The elders of Israel anoint David king in Hebron, he reigns forty years, and he perceives that the LORD has established him (2 Sam. 5:3–4, 12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Covenant with David (Chs. 6–10)'), ('p', 'The LORD tells David through Nathan that He will set up his seed, establish his kingdom and be his seed’s father (2 Sam. 7:8, 12–14, 16, 22, 28).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — David’s Sin and Its Consequences (Chs. 11–20)'), ('p', 'David’s sin displeases the LORD, Nathan says, “Thou art the man,” and David says, “I have sinned against the LORD” (2 Sam. 11:27; 12:7, 13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — David’s Song and the Last Words (Chs. 21–24)'), ('p', 'David praises the LORD as his rock and speaks of an everlasting covenant, and buys the threshingfloor (2 Sam. 22:2, 31; 23:2, 5; 24:14, 24).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 2 Samuel', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The King Anointed</b> — “They anointed David king over Israel” (2 Sam. 5:3).', '<b>The Kingdom Established</b> — “David perceived that the LORD had established him king over Israel, and that he had exalted his kingdom for his people Israel’s sake” (2 Sam. 5:12).', '<b>An Enduring House</b> — “Thine house and thy kingdom shall be established for ever before thee: thy throne shall be established for ever” (2 Sam. 7:16).', '<b>A Son of David</b> — “He shall build an house for my name, and I will stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever” (2 Sam. 7:13).', '<b>An Everlasting Covenant</b> — “He hath made with me an everlasting covenant, ordered in all things, and sure” (2 Sam. 23:5).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 2 Samuel', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how 2 Samuel points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Davidic Covenant</b> — The LORD’s promise of a seed, a house, a kingdom and a throne for ever (2 Sam. 7:12–16) belongs to Israel’s prophetic kingdom hope.', '<b>The Son and the Father</b> — “I will be his father, and he shall be my son” (2 Sam. 7:14) is quoted in the New Testament and is reported here as written.', '<b>David’s Last Words</b> — David says “the Spirit of the LORD spake by me, and his word was in my tongue” (2 Sam. 23:2).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in 2 Samuel', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 2 Samuel speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. 2 Samuel operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 2 Samuel in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', '2 Samuel reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the throne and house of David (2 Sam. 7:12–16)', 'kingdom over Israel in the land (2 Sam. 5:12)', 'the everlasting covenant (2 Sam. 23:5)', 'the altar at the threshingfloor (2 Sam. 24:24)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 2 Samuel that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 2 Samuel contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['David Anointed King — “they anointed David king over Israel.” (2 Sam. 5:3).', 'The Kingdom Established — “the LORD had established him king over Israel.” (2 Sam. 5:12).', 'A Seed After Him — “I will set up thy seed after thee… and I will establish his kingdom.” (2 Sam. 7:12).', 'The Throne for Ever — “I will stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever.” (2 Sam. 7:13).', 'Father and Son — “I will be his father, and he shall be my son.” (2 Sam. 7:14).', 'House and Kingdom — “thy throne shall be established for ever.” (2 Sam. 7:16).', 'The Everlasting Covenant — “he hath made with me an everlasting covenant, ordered in all things, and sure.” (2 Sam. 23:5).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 2 Samuel Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 2 Samuel and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 2 Samuel teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 2 Samuel', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['David Anointed King', '“they anointed David king over Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 5:3'], ['The Kingdom Established', '“the LORD had established him king over Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 5:12'], ['A Seed After Him', '“I will set up thy seed after thee… and I will establish his kingdom.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 7:12'], ['The Throne for Ever', '“I will stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 7:13'], ['Father and Son', '“I will be his father, and he shall be my son.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 7:14'], ['House and Kingdom', '“thy throne shall be established for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 7:16'], ['The Everlasting Covenant', '“he hath made with me an everlasting covenant, ordered in all things, and sure.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Sam. 23:5']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 2 Samuel that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 2 Samuel teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The LORD Is Incomparable', '“there is none like thee, neither is there any God beside thee.”', '2 Sam. 7:22'], ['His Words Are True', '“thou art that God, and thy words be true.”', '2 Sam. 7:28'], ['Sin Is Confessed and Put Away', '“I have sinned against the LORD. And Nathan said unto David, The LORD also hath put away thy sin.”', '2 Sam. 12:13'], ['The LORD Is a Refuge', '“The LORD is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer.”', '2 Sam. 22:2'], ['His Way Is Perfect', '“As for God, his way is perfect.”', '2 Sam. 22:31'], ['Great Mercies', '“for his mercies are great.”', '2 Sam. 24:14']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 2 Samuel without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to 2 Samuel in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Confess sin</b> — David said, “I have sinned” (2 Sam. 12:13).', '<b>Trust the true word of God</b> — His words are true (2 Sam. 7:28).', '<b>Take refuge in the LORD</b> — He is a rock and a fortress (2 Sam. 22:2).', '<b>Rely on His perfect way</b> — His way is perfect (2 Sam. 22:31).', '<b>Fall on His mercies</b> — They are great (2 Sam. 24:14).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 2 Samuel', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 2 Samuel in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['David Becomes King (Chs. 1–5) — Anointed over Israel. <i>(2 Sam. 5:3–4, 12)</i>', 'The Covenant with David (Chs. 6–10) — House, kingdom and throne. <i>(2 Sam. 7:8, 12–14, 16)</i>', 'David’s Sin (Chs. 11–20) — Thou art the man. <i>(2 Sam. 11:27; 12:7, 13)</i>', 'Song and Last Words (Chs. 21–24) — The everlasting covenant. <i>(2 Sam. 22:2, 31; 23:5; 24:14, 24)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 2 Samuel, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward repentance, trust, and humility without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 2 Samuel simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, 2 Samuel stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
